$\\begin{aligned} & =2 \\times 0.5 \\mathrm{~mm}+25 \\times \\frac{0.5}{50}-0.05 \\mathrm{~mm} \\\\ & =1 \\mathrm{~mm}+0.25 \\mathrm{~mm}-0.05 \\mathrm{~mm}=1.20 \\mathrm{~mm}\\end{aligned}$","difficulty":"Easy","source":"Manipal (MET) 2014","_marksId":"67921de01d5b5072da5e9e9c"},{"id":54376,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Experimental Physics","exam":"Engineering","examName":"Manipal (MET)","q":"The length of the second's pendulum is decreased by 0.3 cm when it is shifted to Chennai from London. If the acceleration due to gravity at London is $981 \\mathrm{~cm} / \\mathrm{s}^2$, the acceleration due to gravity at Chennai is","options":["$981 \\mathrm{~cm} / \\mathrm{s}^2$","$978 \\mathrm{~cm} / \\mathrm{s}^2$","$984 \\mathrm{~cm} / \\mathrm{s}^2$","$975 \\mathrm{~cm} / \\mathrm{s}^2$"],"answer":1,"solution":"$L_1=\\frac{g_1 T^2}{4 \\pi^2}=\\frac{g_1}{\\pi^2} ; L_2=\\frac{g_2 T^2}{4 \\pi^2}=\\frac{g_2}{\\pi^2}$<br/>Since, length is decreased, $g_2$ is less than $g_1$.\n$\\therefore \\quad L_1-L_2=\\frac{g_1-g_2}{\\pi^2}$\nor $\\quad\\left(L_1-L_2\\right) \\pi^2=g_1-g_2$\nor $\\quad 0.3 \\times 10=g_1-g_2\\therefore \\quad g_2=981-3=978 \\mathrm{~cm} / \\mathrm{s}^2$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"Manipal (MET) 2012","_marksId":"67921daa1d5b5072da5e9cec"}]}